  "notes": [
    "Test set: 1,317,508 transactions, 1,493 laundering. Warm-up rows and transfers to the same account are excluded because the engine never alerts on them.",
    "Alert threshold 0.0292 is the F1-best threshold on a held-out training day, after Platt scaling of the fused probability on that day. Fusion weights are refitted on the same day by logistic regression; the pack's hand-set weights rank far worse on IBM (see the contrast rows in eval_extra.json) because a detector hit outweighs a confident model score. The LightGBM model is isotonic-calibrated on a separate unsampled day, so legitimate-row subsampling does not inflate it.",
    "The model's strongest feature is whether the payer has paid this payee before. It is computed from past payments only, but IBM's synthetic laundering may favour new payees more than real laundering does.",
    "On IBM the fused score ranks slightly below the LightGBM model alone, so fusion is not claimed to improve accuracy here. Ripple fuses because the detectors and graph layer give each alert its reasons and counterfactuals and cover behaviour the model has never seen; that second point is shown only on simulated traffic (the red-team and one-shot results), not on IBM.",
    "The single-transaction baseline is LightGBM on amount, hour and cash only, given as many alerts as the full system.",
    "Per-pattern precision compares that pattern's laundering rows against all legitimate rows, so it is lower than overall precision.",
    "Pattern tags come from HI-Small_Patterns.txt; laundering rows not listed there are 'untagged'.",
    "8 near-empty tail days at the end of the dataset (mostly laundering rows) are replayed but not used for training or testing."
